  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nA stalled frontal boundary continues to bisect central Kansas with \nextreme heat anticipated south of this boundary while more \nseasonable values can be expected in it's wake. Subtle convergence \nalong the sfc trough axis will continue to promote diurnally driven \nshowers and thunderstorms. The highest probabilities are expected to \narrive after 4 pm when the cap weakens. We continue to see a deeply \nmixed pbl with inverted-v profiles on point soundings supporting \nstrong/damaging winds with the more robust updrafts.\n\nThe stalled boundary will linger over the area on Saturday setting \nthe stage for another day of afternoon/evening showers and \nthunderstorms. We will also see another day of excessive heat \nover southern Kansas on Saturday. The boundary is expected to move \nsouthward Saturday night into Sunday with more seasonable air \narriving across the area on Sunday. The post-frontal airmass will \nbe moist with lower 70 dewpoints leading to less capping during the \nafternoon hours as a subtle wave moves over the area keeping some \nlow pops in the forecast, especially across southeast Kansas closer \nto the frontal zone.\n\nMore seasonable air is expected across the area on Monday and we \nmaintained some low pops as we remain within a moist easterly post-\nfrontal upslope regime. A subtle shortwave trough may help trigger \nsome post-frontal diurnally driven storms on Monday but confidence \nremains fairly low at this time.\n\nThe cooldown will be short-lived with rising heights/increasing \nthickness supporting rising temperatures over much of the area on \nTue and possible Wednesday. Another progressive shortwave trough \nracing eastward over the Northern Plains will drive a frontal \nboundary southward into the Central Plains but this front may become \nstationary leading to low confidence in temperatures on Wednesday. \nThis front may also provide a focus for showers and storms but \nconfidence in the timing remains low. If it arrives slower, the \nchance for showers and storms may be delayed until Wed night with \ncooler air arriving on Thu, a faster fropa would bring higher \nprobabilities for showers and storms to much of the area on \nWednesday. After another brief cooldown on Thu, moderating \ntemperatures are expected to return late in the week as the \nmid/upper ridge amplifies over the Rockies.",
